.TH BMAP_NEXT 3 "2023-04-01" "1.0" "lib/bmap/bmap_next"
.SH NAME
bmap_next_ \- Get the next key from a hash map iteration
.SH SYNOPSIS
#include <lib/bmap/bmap.h>

const char* bmap_next_(bmap_base_t *m, bmap_iter_t *iter);
.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBbmap_next_\fR function retrieves the next key from a hash map iteration. It advances the iterator to the next key and returns the key as a string. If there are no more keys, it returns \fBNULL\fR.

This function is useful for iterating over the keys in a hash map.
.SH ARGUMENTS
\fIm\fR
The hash map being iterated over.
\fIiter\fR
The hash map iterator.
.SH RETURN VALUE
A string representing the next key, or \fBNULL\fR if there are no more keys.
.SH EXAMPLE
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBbmap_next_\fR function:
.nf
#include <lib/bmap/bmap.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    bmap_base_t *m = bmap_init(100);
    if (m == NULL) {
        perror("Failed to initialize hash map");
        return 1;
    }

    // Insert some data into the hash map
    bmap_insert(m, "key1", "value1");
    bmap_insert(m, "key2", "value2");

    // Initialize the iterator
    bmap_iter_t iter = bmap_iter_();

    // Iterate over the hash map
    while (const char *key = bmap_next_(m, &iter)) {
        printf("Key: %s\n", key);
    }

    bmap_deinit_(m);
    return 0;
}
.ne
.fi
